WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:12.490
RYAN MCDOWELL: Joining me today
is Oliver Tweedie from Sky,

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.062
director of data engineering.

00:00:14.062 --> 00:00:15.520
And today we're
going to be talking

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.620
through real-time
streaming analytics

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:22.030
with Dataflow, common
use cases, and patterns.

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:24.160
But before we dive
straight into patterns,

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.840
it's important to
understand streaming

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.850
and why it matters and
a couple of the things

00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.160
that the Apache Beam
SDK and Dataflow do

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.010
to solve some of the common
problems that you may run into.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:37.920
So we can have data
that we're processing.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.100
And that data can be big.

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.530
For instance, we can be
processing from gigabytes

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.840
to terabytes.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:46.680
It could be really, really big.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.045
So we may have huge
amounts of data

00:00:49.045 --> 00:00:51.170
that we want to process
every day and multiple days

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:53.610
that we want to process.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.300
And that data can also
be infinitely big.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.580
So we may have streaming data
that's constantly coming.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.040
It never stops.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.930
And this gives a
problem with processing,

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:06.380
especially with unknown delays.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.960
So one of the more difficult
things with unbounded data

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.580
is unknown delays.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.169
So assuming that every record
has an event timestamp,

00:01:15.169 --> 00:01:17.210
hopefully we would see
the record show up shortly

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.096
after the event that occurred.

00:01:20.096 --> 00:01:21.470
So we're going to
look over here.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.320
The red record here
happened at 8:00 AM.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:26.990
And it showed up in the
system shortly after.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:29.060
The yellow record
happened at 8:00 AM

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.547
and showed up in the
system slightly delayed.

00:01:31.547 --> 00:01:33.380
But then we have this
green record over here

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.630
that has an event
time stamp of 8:00 AM,

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:40.880
but showed up at 2:30, which
is about a six-hour delay.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.700
And there are several reasons
why these delays may happen.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:46.820
The most common case is
actually mobile devices.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.010
So with mobile devices,
you can imagine

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:51.900
that you get on a plane,
you turn your phone

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.680
onto airplane mode,
you play a game

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.050
on your mobile device
the entire plane ride.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.720
And when you get off, you
turn your airplane mode off

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.330
and all those events
shoot up to the cloud.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:05.000
And so you get all these events
into your data pipeline all at

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.670
once because we
had no connectivity

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:10.320
during that period.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.550
Now if we are doing element
wise transforms, such as just

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:17.160
filtering out for the
yellow records here,

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.420
that's relatively simple.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.740
We don't care about
processing time or event time.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.212
Its easy because its a
continuous transform.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:31.050
But when we get to grouping data
via processing time windows,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.930
so let's say we want
to do an aggregation.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:37.070
We want to count the number of
events that happened per user.

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.440
For batch it's easy
because it's bounded.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.690
We just read the
input stream, and then

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:44.790
as soon as there's no more
data, we stop processing

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.300
and we calculate the
result and emit it.

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.905
For streaming we can't do that.

00:02:48.905 --> 00:02:53.030
You never run out of data,
so there's no way of saying,

00:02:53.030 --> 00:02:57.716
this is the time where I output
the count that I've seen.

00:02:57.716 --> 00:02:59.340
So most commonly what
people want to do

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.600
is group data into
processing time windows,

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.590
or windows rather, and divide
data up into small chunks

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:08.510
so that they can
compute aggregations

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:11.750
over that chunk of time
and then output results.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:15.380
So initial approach is to
use processing time windows.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.390
Essentially when a
job sees a record,

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:19.770
it looks at the wall clock,
it does that for a minute,

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:20.930
and then outputs a record.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:26.840
But what we really
may want to do

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.000
is actually group
that by event time.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.640
So instead of viewing something
as a time on the wall clock,

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:37.840
viewing data as a stream,
and group by our event

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.860
timestamps within our data.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.120
And output them as we see them.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.180
This allows us to
perform calculations

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:48.290
over the event time interval
which the events have occurred.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:51.170
So essentially what we do
is we take the input, which

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.690
is all the events occurring
and processing time,

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:57.890
we regroup them and shuffle them
to group them into event time

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.120
and calculate meaningful
analytics over our data.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:04.530
So I mentioned windows.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:05.690
What is a window?

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:10.050
A window is just a function
to divide data into chunks.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:13.080
Within the Apache
Beam SDK and Dataflow,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.960
we provide several different
window types to slice

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:18.310
and dice your data
in different ways.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:21.510
So for instance, here's some
of the more common ones--

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.464
fixed windows and
sliding windows.

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:26.130
So a fixed window,
you could think of it

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:30.450
as processing data from like
one to two, two to three,

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:31.852
so on and so forth.

00:04:31.852 --> 00:04:33.810
And so you're pretty much
dividing up your data

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:37.560
into time chunks and then
grouping it and providing

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.140
some aggregation on that data.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:41.820
Sliding windows, you
can think of those

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:44.220
as overlapping fixed windows.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.370
So an event that happened
may actually end up

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.020
in multiple different windows.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:51.930
So say that I had a sliding
window that was an hour,

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:55.290
and it slides every
minute, I essentially

00:04:55.290 --> 00:05:02.700
have windows that end up being
12:01 to 1:01, 12:02 to 1:02,

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:03.605
so on and so forth.

00:05:03.605 --> 00:05:06.000
And so I could have a lot
of overlapping windows.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.360
And this is useful when you're
doing things like analysis

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.910
on maybe the input
rate of the data

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:16.380
or calculating how
many events that I've

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:20.070
seen in the past
minute or 10 minutes

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:21.540
for a particular event type.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.120
In Beam we also support
data-dependent windows.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:34.140
So what if I wanted to group by
something that was in my event

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:35.410
itself?

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:41.680
So I want to create windows
for users, for instance.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.380
This is what we
would call Sessions.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:47.070
So every key here ends
up in a different window.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:51.240
Sessions are defined by whenever
there is a burst of activity.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.590
I want to group that into
a window for processing

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:55.950
and calculate
statistics over that.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.630
And then when there's no
data for a period of time,

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:01.080
such as a gap, I'm going
to close that window

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.580
and fire it off.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.206
This is a common thing you
may want to do in analytics.

00:06:06.206 --> 00:06:08.580
And it's supported out of the
box and Dataflow and Apache

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:09.080
Beam.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:17.380
So I've talked a little bit
about the windowing semantics

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:19.600
and how we would divide
our data up and maybe do

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.320
computations over it within a
real-time analytics pipeline.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:25.360
But when does the
answer come out?

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:26.860
This is problematic
because you have

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.130
to know when to emit
the result for the data

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:33.520
that you're seeing
for that window.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.850
By default, what we have is
something called a watermark.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:39.610
The watermark tries
to know when we are

00:06:39.610 --> 00:06:41.860
done with data for a window.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.230
So if the watermark says
12 o'clock, it means,

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:45.850
to the best of our
knowledge, there

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:49.720
is no more data left
before 12 o'clock.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:53.410
And we can calculate the
result and emit it downstream.

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:56.440
For external sources, it has
to be somewhat heuristic.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:58.240
So, for instance,
for Pub/Sub, we

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.210
can actually do a read-ahead
of the Pub/Sub buffer

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:05.380
to essentially look at is
there any more messages left

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.540
before a certain period
of time and then know

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:11.080
that we can effectively
emit the results.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:16.460
Now going back to the mobile
phone on a plane example,

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:20.080
you could have someone who
has data from 11 o'clock

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:20.810
on their phone.

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.200
They don't have any
internet connectivity.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:25.000
And obviously we
can't know about that.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.570
So that actually
becomes late data.

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:29.560
So if we look at the
heuristic watermark here,

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:32.440
essentially you can see that
while it does pretty well,

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:35.560
it doesn't catch the
nine that happens

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:37.420
a lot later than we expected.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:40.210
For the perfect
watermark, it is perfect.

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:42.451
It knows about everything,
including events

00:07:42.451 --> 00:07:43.700
that may happen in the future.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:48.820
So what happens when
you have late data?

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:51.130
In the Beam SDK, we have
a way of saying that I

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.020
want to process late data.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.910
You can add triggering
to your windows

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:57.930
to indicate you want to
process that late data.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:00.650
And there are several different
ways of processing it.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:05.110
So for instance, using
accumulating processing

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:07.810
means that if I have
late data show up,

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:12.350
I want to add that late data
to my previous aggregation.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:14.200
So for instance, if I
was counting elements

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:18.730
and I had to count to 50 before,
and that the data shows up,

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:19.880
I give--

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:21.730
I fire off that late
data, and I get back

00:08:21.730 --> 00:08:25.120
a result of 52 instead
of the original 50,

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.850
when two late elements show up.

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:31.900
I can also say I want
to discard late data--

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:34.570
or discard fired panes, rather.

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:40.030
So essentially if I wanted to do
this count and compute the sum,

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:42.340
I could have 50 elements
that show up on time.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:44.470
I could have two elements
that show up late.

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:48.730
And as opposed to recalculating
the result and firing off 52,

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:51.090
I may say forget about the
50 that I had previously

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:53.340
calculated, I only want
to get the count of two

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.140
and discard those
previous fired panes.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:58.090
So in this other
animation, you can actually

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:02.710
see some of our speculative
in late firings.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:05.260
So in this case, the
heuristic watermark

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:10.270
is actually firing speculatively
for records that show up

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:12.730
before the watermark,
and then also catching

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:15.770
those late records for
a given period of time.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:17.440
So we're actually
able to capture

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:20.260
a lot of that data
that does not show up

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:21.260
when we would expect it.

00:09:24.737 --> 00:09:26.820
So that was a little bit
about streaming, and sort

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.590
of the common
challenges, and how

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:30.840
Dataflow and the Apache
Beam SDK helps address

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:32.136
some of those challenges.

00:09:32.136 --> 00:09:33.510
For the subsequent
section, we're

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:36.570
actually going to talk about
building a pipeline using

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:38.100
some common patterns.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:41.790
So from just simple
element wise transforms

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:45.452
to grouping aggregations
and that type stuff.

00:09:45.452 --> 00:09:47.910
For this we're actually going
to use the scenario that I've

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:51.630
been alluding to previously
of capturing events

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:54.810
and analyzing them
for a mobile game.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:56.880
So let's imagine we
have a mobile game,

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:59.610
and we want to build
a real-time analytics

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:03.854
pipeline to analyze those game
events that may be happening.

00:10:03.854 --> 00:10:05.520
And we want our game
to be popular, too.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:08.640
So we want to be able to
handle these events at scale

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:10.590
and be able to do
interesting things

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:13.200
like compute aggregations
over how many events

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.410
that we're seeing per
user or analyze the user

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:17.700
behavior within the game app.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:24.440
We'll start out with
exactly-once processing.

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:27.180
So starting to build this
pipeline from the most simple

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:32.130
case, we may have our gaming
devices publishing up data

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:37.110
into Pub/Sub, then Dataflow
processing that data,

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:39.690
and then BigQuery
finally landing that data

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:42.060
into our data warehouse.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:45.420
We're using Pub/Sub because it's
a reliable, globally available

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.170
message delivery service.

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:50.640
And we expect our game to be
popular worldwide, so why not?

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:53.190
Now if we're analyzing
user behavior,

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:54.900
duplicates which
may enter our system

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:57.270
may throw off our analytics.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:01.320
So if I'm calculating counts,
if I'm doing averages,

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:03.330
if I allow duplicates
into my system,

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:06.300
I'm going to throw off the
analytics that I'm doing

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:11.534
and, thus, make them
substantially less valuable.

00:11:11.534 --> 00:11:13.450
And this is also critical
in other industries,

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:15.090
such as retail or finance.

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:19.980
So imagine I have a POS system,
which is generating transaction

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:20.520
events.

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:21.978
I obviously wouldn't
want to double

00:11:21.978 --> 00:11:24.750
count those transactions,
and same thing with trades

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:27.440
or holdings.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:30.520
In this example, zooming
in onto Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:33.580
you may think that it
may cause a problem

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:36.970
because it has at-least
once delivery semantics.

00:11:36.970 --> 00:11:38.680
So inherently by
using Pub/Sub, we

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:42.760
may receive duplicates
within our system.

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:45.350
Here the colors indicate
unique keys for the records.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:49.280
So we have red, yellow,
green coming in,

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:53.130
and we may have duplicate
records that come out.

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:55.290
However, when combining
Pub/Sub with Dataflow,

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:58.770
we're actually able to achieve
this exactly-once semantics

00:11:58.770 --> 00:11:59.610
within our pipeline.

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:03.827
And it's with relatively
little effort.

00:12:03.827 --> 00:12:05.410
So we're going to
zoom in a little bit

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:07.480
on the records coming
into our pipeline.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.010
It may look like this.

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:12.640
We have attributes in a payload.

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:15.850
That comprises the message
that's going into Pub/Sub.

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:17.980
Attributes are key value pairs.

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:20.290
You can think of it as
a header on the message.

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:23.140
It allows you to sort
of inspect the message

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:24.640
and maybe route it
in different ways

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:27.850
or perform other operations
without having to actually

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:29.580
deserialize the payload.

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:32.560
And the payload contains
the serialized payload

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:33.260
of our message.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:35.990
In this case, we're
publishing JSON events up

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:38.260
into our Pub/Sub topic.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:41.510
And you'll see that we
have a unique identifier

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:45.444
within the message
payload, an event ID.

00:12:45.444 --> 00:12:47.110
So the first step to
actually processing

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:51.070
this in exactly-once fashion is
that we'll promote this event

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:53.560
ID into our attributes.

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:56.090
And this allows us
to instruct Dataflow

00:12:56.090 --> 00:12:58.145
to direct to it
within our pipeline

00:12:58.145 --> 00:12:59.770
and start deduplicating
these messages.

00:13:02.272 --> 00:13:03.980
So we take a look at
our example pipeline

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:06.440
here for our game events.

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:08.570
It may look like the
following, so essentially we

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:10.430
have a Pub/Sub I/O transform.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:12.710
It's reading some data
from a subscription.

00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:17.400
And then it's passing down data
downstream to our transforms.

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.640
So the first step was
to add in that event ID

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:21.390
into our attributes.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:25.010
Now we need to instruct Dataflow
to react to that event ID.

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:26.720
And that's relatively
simple here.

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:31.280
As opposed to just having a
very vanilla transform with just

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:36.260
specifying the subscription, we
add in this .withIdAttribute.

00:13:36.260 --> 00:13:38.540
And this allows
Dataflow to know,

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:40.340
I'm going to look
at this event ID

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:41.930
and deduplicate on
that as messages

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:43.950
come into the pipeline.

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:45.800
Now this will
deduplicate messages that

00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:47.270
are being caused by Pub/Sub.

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:50.810
So any messages that are
duplicated as part of Pub/Sub's

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:52.550
at-least once delivery
semantics will

00:13:52.550 --> 00:13:55.610
be deduplicated automatically
within the pipeline.

00:13:55.610 --> 00:13:57.404
There's also other
areas in which

00:13:57.404 --> 00:13:58.820
we can receive
duplicate messages,

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:03.390
such as a publisher maybe
publishing a message twice.

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:05.915
This will also pick
that up as well,

00:14:05.915 --> 00:14:08.040
but it will only do that
within a 10-minute window.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:12.110
So we don't keep around
the event ID deduplication

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:14.120
table for the past 10 minutes.

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:16.850
So if you had a
publisher maybe going--

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:18.740
publishing a message
at minute one,

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:22.460
and then they publish the same
duplicate message at hour one,

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:25.400
minute one, you would then have
to account for that elsewhere

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:27.770
in your pipeline, such as
using a unique transform.

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:35.230
So we're achieving exactly-once
semantics within our pipeline.

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:36.760
The next thing we
may want to do is

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:39.640
think about how we
deal with bad data.

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:41.890
In order to deal with bad
data coming into our system,

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:44.390
we're actually going to add
some things to our architecture,

00:14:44.390 --> 00:14:48.420
such as Pub/Sub to handle a
dead letter topic and Cloud

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.437
Functions then to alert
on messages which end up

00:14:50.437 --> 00:14:51.520
in that dead letter topic.

00:14:56.232 --> 00:14:57.940
So what does this look
like in actuality?

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:01.420
So we have this message
being published in.

00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:05.510
You can notice that this message
is malformed in some way.

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:08.900
We don't have a closing
tag on our JSON object.

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:14.060
The event ID is no longer
in the proper format.

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:17.560
So we were expecting
a string, and now it

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:20.690
doesn't have the
quotes around it.

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:23.500
So by default, if this
was to enter our pipeline,

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:27.130
it's going to fail when we try
to parse these messages, right?

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:30.160
In Dataflow, we'll actually
try to reprocess those messages

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:32.200
forever in streaming
mode because we

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:34.310
don't want to lose that data.

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:36.850
So if that data fails and
it throws an exception

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:38.980
we don't want to just
drop it on the floor.

00:15:38.980 --> 00:15:42.610
So we'll continue to
reprocess it forever

00:15:42.610 --> 00:15:44.600
or until you tell us
what to do with it,

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:46.420
such as updating your
pipeline or creating

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:48.836
a new path within your pipeline
to deal with the bad data.

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:54.570
So what do we do?

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:57.280
We can actually edit
our ParDo function.

00:15:57.280 --> 00:16:00.012
So each one of those
boxes is a ParDo function.

00:16:00.012 --> 00:16:01.970
Essentially you could
think of a ParDo function

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:04.920
as almost like a
map in MapReduce.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:08.770
ParDo function has a process
element method, where

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:10.610
it performs the computation.

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:13.970
And so, as opposed to just
performing our computation

00:16:13.970 --> 00:16:15.860
and outputting
records, what we'll do

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:17.780
is we'll wrap it in
a try-catch block

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:20.060
and then we'll output
[? filled ?] records

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:23.294
for any exception to
our dead letter tag.

00:16:23.294 --> 00:16:24.710
And that dead
letter tag allows us

00:16:24.710 --> 00:16:27.432
to then retrieve those messages
later on in our pipeline

00:16:27.432 --> 00:16:29.390
and then do something
with them, such as output

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:30.515
them to a persistent store.

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:38.640
So if we were to go back to our
pipeline here, in this case,

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:40.940
we are now redirecting
our messages

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:42.710
to a different location.

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:44.090
We're taking that
dead letter tag

00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:48.620
and we are then routing it
to both BigQuery and Pub/Sub.

00:16:48.620 --> 00:16:50.700
We're routing to
BigQuery and Pub/Sub

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:52.580
because we want our
support team to be

00:16:52.580 --> 00:16:56.900
able to analyze those bad
data, to be able to inspect it

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:59.000
within BigQuery.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:00.980
And then we also want
to output it to Pub/Sub

00:17:00.980 --> 00:17:03.047
for ease of reprocessing
and alerting

00:17:03.047 --> 00:17:04.130
within our Cloud Function.

00:17:08.119 --> 00:17:11.780
And so in this example, this is
an example of a BigQuery table

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:15.800
that you may see for dead letter
processing, where we're storing

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:18.950
maybe the event timestamp,
the payload in bytes,

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:21.440
and maybe try to convert
the payload to a string

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:24.230
to show more data
about that payload

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:25.950
so someone has more context--

00:17:25.950 --> 00:17:29.450
so the attributes, and the error
message, and the stack trays.

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:31.550
And this way, someone
can go in there,

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:34.010
see what the problem is, and
then hopefully rectify it.

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:40.701
So we've added handling for
bad data within our pipeline,

00:17:40.701 --> 00:17:42.700
but we also may actually
have another case which

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:46.635
may cause failures, and
that is schema changes.

00:17:46.635 --> 00:17:48.760
So for a mobile game,
imagine that we have partners

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:50.530
that are publishing this data.

00:17:50.530 --> 00:17:52.440
So our partners are
publishing this data,

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:55.690
and they may want to update
the schema at some point.

00:17:55.690 --> 00:17:59.450
And we need to react to
that within our pipeline.

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:03.520
So we may need to add additional
fields within our pipeline

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:05.219
dynamically.

00:18:05.219 --> 00:18:06.760
Now we could do this
not dynamically.

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:09.730
We could have coordination, like
we could call up the partner

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:11.146
and say, we're
going to coordinate

00:18:11.146 --> 00:18:15.670
a release on this date and have
a lot of operational overhead.

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:17.650
But we actually want to
dynamically handle this

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:21.850
because then we're free from
that operational overhead

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:25.520
of having to manage it.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:27.130
So looking back at
our messages, we

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:29.320
may have the message
on the left, which

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:33.580
is the yellow message, which is
coming in just as we expected

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:35.130
in the schema that we expected.

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:38.130
But we also may have a
message on the right,

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:42.270
like the green message here,
that has an additional field.

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:44.410
So the partner may
have added a device

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:47.470
IP or some contextual
information that's

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:49.060
useful for them.

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:51.310
And we want to capture that.

00:18:51.310 --> 00:18:52.950
But if they added
an additional field,

00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:55.325
we may fail on our
insert into BigQuery.

00:18:55.325 --> 00:18:56.700
So we're going to
have to account

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:59.890
for that within our pipeline.

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:02.819
So there's a couple of things
that BigQuery and Dataflow--

00:19:02.819 --> 00:19:04.860
are a couple of features
of BigQuery and Dataflow

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:07.700
that allow us to do
this fairly efficiently.

00:19:07.700 --> 00:19:09.390
BigQuery allows
for schema changes

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:12.540
which are additive
within the same table.

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:17.809
So schema changes happen
online and require no downtime.

00:19:17.809 --> 00:19:19.350
And the BigQuery
sync within Dataflow

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:23.890
allows the retrieval
of field inserts.

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:25.390
Combining all these
things together,

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:29.040
we can actually achieve
this dynamic mutating schema

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:31.900
within our pipeline.

00:19:31.900 --> 00:19:34.350
If we were to look at
our pipeline again,

00:19:34.350 --> 00:19:38.850
we may add in the
getFailedInserts inserts

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:41.910
when we're writing
out to BigQuery.

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:44.040
This allows us to retrieve
everything that has

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:46.650
failed insertion to BigQuery.

00:19:46.650 --> 00:19:48.450
Then we may invoke
a function which

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:50.760
dynamically mutates our schema.

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:55.116
So everything that failed
to insert, inspect it, add

00:19:55.116 --> 00:19:56.490
the additional
field to BigQuery,

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:59.610
and then pass it downstream,
and then finally writing

00:19:59.610 --> 00:20:02.040
those mutated records out.

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:04.230
Now this allows us to do
this fairly efficiently.

00:20:04.230 --> 00:20:06.030
So alternatively what
we could have done

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:08.640
is we could have inspected
every single message which

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:10.137
enters our pipeline.

00:20:10.137 --> 00:20:11.970
And that's fairly
inefficient because you're

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:14.010
doing an operation on
a bunch of elements

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:15.790
that you may not need to, right?

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:19.120
The schema change may
only happen occasionally.

00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:22.600
And so you're just adding
overhead to your pipeline.

00:20:22.600 --> 00:20:25.530
So as opposed to doing that,
reacting to the failed inserts,

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:27.660
and then mutating the
schema dynamically,

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:29.790
and then rewriting those
records out to BigQuery

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:32.190
allows us to have
efficient, dynamic mutating

00:20:32.190 --> 00:20:35.280
schemas within Dataflow.

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:37.560
So if we were to look
at our pipeline again,

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:38.610
how does this look?

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:42.080
We have messages that
may have mutated schema.

00:20:42.080 --> 00:20:44.520
And essentially we've
added a whole new path here

00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:48.510
in which errors come
out, and then we validate

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:50.310
and we mutate that
schema, and then

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:52.660
we re-output it to BigQuery.

00:20:52.660 --> 00:20:57.690
And so without having to have
this complexity of managing

00:20:57.690 --> 00:21:00.300
changes with our partners,
we're able to react

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:02.513
to it automatically
within our pipeline.

00:21:06.220 --> 00:21:09.700
Another common use case is
real-time denormalization.

00:21:09.700 --> 00:21:14.110
So in order provide a summary
view in our data warehouse,

00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:18.502
we may want to look up reference
data before we output events.

00:21:18.502 --> 00:21:19.960
In our mobile gaming
use case, this

00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:22.810
could be looking up reference
data like on the user

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:26.830
or on the partner, adding
contextual information that

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:31.250
will help us do
our analysis later.

00:21:31.250 --> 00:21:33.370
In order to
accomplish this, we'll

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:36.860
need to add an additional data
store to our architecture.

00:21:36.860 --> 00:21:40.640
In this case, we choose Bigtable
because of the scalability

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:42.860
and latency requirements
that we may have.

00:21:42.860 --> 00:21:46.250
If our game is popular, we
may be processing thousands,

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:49.940
hundreds of thousands, or
million events per second,

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:53.830
so we need something
that can scale to that.

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:58.630
Before we implement
the per-record lookups,

00:21:58.630 --> 00:22:01.910
we want to consider both the
performance of the database,

00:22:01.910 --> 00:22:04.449
so if we're doing
this within GCP,

00:22:04.449 --> 00:22:06.490
we're going to prefer
Bigtable or Datastore to do

00:22:06.490 --> 00:22:10.255
these per-record lookups because
they'll give us the performance

00:22:10.255 --> 00:22:11.130
metrics that we need.

00:22:11.130 --> 00:22:12.720
If we were to reach
out to BigQuery,

00:22:12.720 --> 00:22:14.595
it would be quite a bit
slower because you're

00:22:14.595 --> 00:22:16.570
talking on the orders
of seconds as opposed

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:18.770
to on the orders of
tens of milliseconds

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:21.370
for each individual look up.

00:22:21.370 --> 00:22:23.530
And we also want something
that scalable, too.

00:22:23.530 --> 00:22:27.310
Because if we put, let's say, a
single postgres instance to do

00:22:27.310 --> 00:22:30.250
our lookups, Dataflow
is like a cannon

00:22:30.250 --> 00:22:31.930
we shoot at GCP products.

00:22:31.930 --> 00:22:36.430
And so you can easily make
a single instance fall over.

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:38.230
So we want to keep
that in mind when we're

00:22:38.230 --> 00:22:41.190
doing our per-record lookups.

00:22:41.190 --> 00:22:43.980
And finally, we
also want to utilize

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:48.360
the right areas to initialize
and tear down our connections.

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:51.630
ParDos within Dataflow
have a lifecycle.

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:53.899
And I'll speak to that
lifecycle in just a second.

00:22:53.899 --> 00:22:55.440
But essentially, we
want to make sure

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:57.856
that we're initializing and
tearing down these connections

00:22:57.856 --> 00:22:59.860
in the proper place
within our pipeline,

00:22:59.860 --> 00:23:02.840
so within the @Setup
method of the Do function

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:05.370
and within the @Teardown
method of the Do function

00:23:05.370 --> 00:23:08.330
to close the connection.

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:10.820
So we're going to look
at the ParDo lifecycle.

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:12.950
This is sort of a
crude representation

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:14.480
of what it might look like.

00:23:14.480 --> 00:23:18.050
We have @Setup, StartBundle,
and ProcessElement.

00:23:18.050 --> 00:23:21.560
And then we also have your
FinishBundle and Teardown.

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:26.375
So where do we do our connection
initialization and teardown?

00:23:26.375 --> 00:23:28.250
We're going to do that
in setup and teardown,

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:29.857
just like it sounds like.

00:23:29.857 --> 00:23:31.940
And the reason being is
because it's executed once

00:23:31.940 --> 00:23:33.540
per DoFn instance.

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:37.580
So as these functions are
distributed across your worker

00:23:37.580 --> 00:23:40.100
pool within your job,
it's executed once,

00:23:40.100 --> 00:23:43.880
and so you don't have a ton of
setup and teardown connections.

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:46.530
It is rather expensive
to create new connections

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:49.790
and tear them down all the time.

00:23:49.790 --> 00:23:53.780
StartBundle and FinishBundle
are executed once

00:23:53.780 --> 00:23:56.900
per bundle of records,
so within the pipeline,

00:23:56.900 --> 00:23:59.480
Dataflow will actually
bundle records up and then

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:01.790
process them to each transform.

00:24:01.790 --> 00:24:04.430
And this allows it
to be more efficient

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:06.830
and not have to
materialize the output

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:09.080
for each individual record.

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:10.880
And we could have
done it here, but this

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:13.460
would be executed a lot more
frequently because there's

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:15.543
going to be a lot more
bundles within the pipeline

00:24:15.543 --> 00:24:17.480
than there are
instances of the DoFn.

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:20.750
So this would be a decent place
to actually initialize and tear

00:24:20.750 --> 00:24:23.120
down our connections,
but it would not

00:24:23.120 --> 00:24:26.290
provide the performance
that we would like.

00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:28.350
And finally, we have the
ProcessElement method,

00:24:28.350 --> 00:24:30.970
and this is executed
once per worker.

00:24:30.970 --> 00:24:34.060
If you do really expensive
things in the ProcessElement

00:24:34.060 --> 00:24:36.920
method, this is where you
can slow down your pipeline.

00:24:36.920 --> 00:24:40.270
So for instance, initializing
connections and tearing them

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:43.600
down, if you're doing that
for each individual record

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:45.880
and you're processing
records at hundreds

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:48.190
of thousands or
millions per second,

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:50.327
you are going slow down
your pipeline quite a bit.

00:24:50.327 --> 00:24:51.910
And so that's why
it's pretty critical

00:24:51.910 --> 00:24:55.300
to connect and tear
down your connections

00:24:55.300 --> 00:24:58.120
to do these external
lookups in the correct place

00:24:58.120 --> 00:25:00.840
within your DoFn.

00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:03.480
So zooming in on
our pipeline again,

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:06.660
we want to enrich data
from an external store.

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:08.820
And essentially
we just have added

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:13.320
a couple of different methods
to the Enrich Messages DoFn.

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:15.420
Essentially now we're
in the @Setup method.

00:25:15.420 --> 00:25:17.130
We're initialising
that connection.

00:25:17.130 --> 00:25:21.030
We're calling a method to enrich
the record, which will then

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:23.767
call out to our
external data store.

00:25:23.767 --> 00:25:25.350
And then in our
Teardown method, we're

00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:27.990
closing down that connection.

00:25:27.990 --> 00:25:30.930
And this allows us to have
efficient per-record lookups

00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:34.322
within our pipeline.

00:25:34.322 --> 00:25:37.470
And if we were to look at what
our pipeline looks like as we

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:40.740
add in this functionality,
essentially we

00:25:40.740 --> 00:25:46.260
have this additional lookup
here connecting to Bigtable,

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:48.420
doing the per-record
lookup, enriching the data

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:51.810
with either user data or partner
data for our game events,

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:53.520
and then passing
that data downstream.

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:02.580
So now that we've done a lot
of the common functionality

00:26:02.580 --> 00:26:04.110
that we may have
to our pipeline,

00:26:04.110 --> 00:26:08.520
we may want to perform
analysis on the data that's

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:10.170
moving through.

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:12.030
So this is where we
may want to utilize

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:16.190
some of the windowing techniques
that I had mentioned earlier.

00:26:16.190 --> 00:26:20.630
So here we don't need to add
any additional components

00:26:20.630 --> 00:26:22.630
to our architecture because
windowing is handled

00:26:22.630 --> 00:26:25.360
within Dataflow itself,
so there's nothing

00:26:25.360 --> 00:26:26.560
that needs to be added here.

00:26:30.140 --> 00:26:33.160
And for our analysis,
as I alluded to earlier,

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:36.900
we want to do some analysis to
perform, how long has the user

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:38.820
spent playing the game?

00:26:38.820 --> 00:26:41.340
We may also want
to possibly know

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:43.050
when the user stopped playing.

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:46.050
We may want to do things
like, for a session,

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:48.370
group together all of the
events and then compute

00:26:48.370 --> 00:26:51.930
and output one aggregate record
that contains an array using

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:56.220
BigQuery's nested and repeated
fields to our advantage.

00:26:56.220 --> 00:26:58.800
And so here we'll
actually do the,

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:01.800
how long has the user
spent playing a game?

00:27:01.800 --> 00:27:04.950
Now if we were to
use fixed windows,

00:27:04.950 --> 00:27:07.380
we inherently have
problems because we

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:10.870
may have family members
that use the same device.

00:27:10.870 --> 00:27:13.800
And so if we were just
to group by fixed windows

00:27:13.800 --> 00:27:17.040
of a period of time, from like
12:00 to 1:00 or something

00:27:17.040 --> 00:27:18.570
like that, we may
actually end up

00:27:18.570 --> 00:27:21.820
with different individual
users within that time period.

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:24.660
And so that would
throw off our analysis

00:27:24.660 --> 00:27:28.104
of the count of messages that
were seen per user or even

00:27:28.104 --> 00:27:29.520
any additional
analysis that we're

00:27:29.520 --> 00:27:32.320
doing on the user behavior.

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:35.520
So this would have
incorrect correlations.

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:37.920
What we really want is
to utilize the sessioning

00:27:37.920 --> 00:27:39.900
that I had mentioned earlier.

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:42.630
Because with session windows,
what we would achieve

00:27:42.630 --> 00:27:44.110
is what we really want.

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:48.030
We basically group together
the events by a key,

00:27:48.030 --> 00:27:50.910
and that key being a
user ID, for instance.

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:52.740
And then we're able
to get all the events

00:27:52.740 --> 00:27:56.410
for a particular
user into one window

00:27:56.410 --> 00:28:00.540
and then compute aggregations
and do analysis on that window

00:28:00.540 --> 00:28:02.272
before passing that
data downstream.

00:28:02.272 --> 00:28:04.730
And so this gives us exactly
the subdivisions that we want.

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:11.950
So looking at our pipeline
code, we window the sessions

00:28:11.950 --> 00:28:13.910
with a gap of five minutes.

00:28:13.910 --> 00:28:17.200
So essentially, we are going
to look at bursts of activity,

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:20.140
and whenever we do not see
activity for five minutes

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:23.120
we'll close that window
and we'll fire it off.

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:25.390
And that will trigger the
aggregation downstream

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:27.550
in our processing.

00:28:27.550 --> 00:28:30.080
After that, we're going
to key by the user ID.

00:28:30.080 --> 00:28:32.860
So essentially we're going to
take that game event coming in,

00:28:32.860 --> 00:28:35.550
and then we're going
to get the user ID.

00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:38.860
And this actually
creates a key value pair

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:40.000
that we can then group by.

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:41.560
So essentially we're
keying these records

00:28:41.560 --> 00:28:43.476
before we group them so
we can get the records

00:28:43.476 --> 00:28:45.460
into the right place.

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:47.572
Then we're going to
group by those records,

00:28:47.572 --> 00:28:49.030
so we're going to
group by that key

00:28:49.030 --> 00:28:51.250
that we have with
the game events.

00:28:51.250 --> 00:28:55.150
And that's basically going
to create a grouping of a key

00:28:55.150 --> 00:28:57.490
to an array of all
the records that

00:28:57.490 --> 00:29:00.420
map to that key
within that session.

00:29:00.420 --> 00:29:03.310
And then finally, we're going
to call a method, or a class,

00:29:03.310 --> 00:29:05.480
which is going to
analyze our events.

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:10.600
So within this class, we can do
the counting or the averaging

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:11.470
of those records.

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:15.360
We can do the behavior
analysis that we want to.

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:17.470
And really, we can
do any analysis

00:29:17.470 --> 00:29:19.480
that we see fit
for our use case.

00:29:22.090 --> 00:29:24.100
So now looking at
our updated pipeline,

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:27.120
we have this whole new path.

00:29:27.120 --> 00:29:30.930
After converting to rows, we
output to session windows.

00:29:30.930 --> 00:29:31.890
We key by the user.

00:29:31.890 --> 00:29:34.380
We group by the key, and
then we analyze the events.

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:38.790
And it all happens before we
pass it downstream to BigQuery.

00:29:38.790 --> 00:29:42.090
So in this example,
what we did is

00:29:42.090 --> 00:29:46.950
we deduplicated records
using exactly-once.

00:29:46.950 --> 00:29:49.380
We handled bad data.

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:51.740
We handled mutating data.

00:29:51.740 --> 00:29:55.950
We handled enriching data in
real time with these records.

00:29:55.950 --> 00:30:00.120
And then we also were able
to then window these records

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:03.420
into meaningful windows
for our use case

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:06.870
and compute analytics
that we preferred.

00:30:06.870 --> 00:30:10.680
And so now I'm going to
hand it over to Oliver

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:13.132
to talk us through
Dataflow at Sky.

00:30:13.132 --> 00:30:14.840
OLIVER TWEEDIE: Just
to introduce myself.

00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:15.631
I'm Oliver Tweedie.

00:30:15.631 --> 00:30:18.300
I'm director of data
engineering at Sky.

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:21.030
And for those people who
have not heard of Sky,

00:30:21.030 --> 00:30:24.340
we're one of the leading
TV companies in Europe.

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:28.760
We've got 22 million subscribers
across the UK, Italy, Germany,

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:29.260
and Austria.

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:33.330
And we're a quad play provider,
so we do TV, broadband, phone,

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:35.300
and mobile services.

00:30:35.300 --> 00:30:37.920
And we selected Google
in 2017 primarily

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:39.450
for its data capabilities.

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:43.920
So we spent about four months
getting Google Cloud set up

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:46.500
for Sky and designing
our target architecture.

00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:49.980
And we've been up and running
and live for eight months now.

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:53.220
So we consider GCP to be
a great data and analytics

00:30:53.220 --> 00:30:56.550
platform as a service.

00:30:56.550 --> 00:30:59.730
So today I am going
to talk about Sky Q.

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:03.500
Now Sky Q is our
flagship TV platform.

00:31:03.500 --> 00:31:06.930
It's a TV box, and we think
it offers one of the best TV

00:31:06.930 --> 00:31:11.610
experiences in the UK in terms
of quality of the platform

00:31:11.610 --> 00:31:14.550
and the range of content
available for the box.

00:31:14.550 --> 00:31:16.911
So Sky Q has a satellite tuner.

00:31:16.911 --> 00:31:17.910
It does video on demand.

00:31:17.910 --> 00:31:20.160
It does live streaming.

00:31:20.160 --> 00:31:23.250
It does HD and UHD content.

00:31:23.250 --> 00:31:27.270
And what we do is we collect
diagnostic data from Sky Q

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:29.490
boxes in every customer's
home, and that's

00:31:29.490 --> 00:31:31.530
sort of three million
boxes at the moment

00:31:31.530 --> 00:31:33.840
as live in the field.

00:31:33.840 --> 00:31:36.000
So we want to collect
diagnostic data so we

00:31:36.000 --> 00:31:38.610
can understand user journeys.

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:40.590
We want to be able to
optimize those user

00:31:40.590 --> 00:31:43.920
journeys so our
customers can find the TV

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:45.480
content they love easily.

00:31:45.480 --> 00:31:50.370
So that means fewest clicks
or shortest searches,

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:54.870
easy-to-navigate UI,
promotions, and personalization.

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:57.750
So for example, someone
clicks on the Home menu,

00:31:57.750 --> 00:32:01.350
they go to Movies, they select
a genre, they find the movie,

00:32:01.350 --> 00:32:03.360
they press Play.

00:32:03.360 --> 00:32:06.240
So our original solution
for Sky Q analytics

00:32:06.240 --> 00:32:07.590
was based on a Hadoop cluster.

00:32:07.590 --> 00:32:11.590
It was a fixed-size
cluster on-prem.

00:32:11.590 --> 00:32:14.820
And Sky Q, great product, really
successful, but the problem

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:19.080
was that we had to deal
with lots of peaks of data.

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:24.430
So TV diagnostic data has
large peaks, so in the UK,

00:32:24.430 --> 00:32:27.630
we don't have the weather of
California, unfortunately.

00:32:27.630 --> 00:32:28.530
It rains a lot.

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:30.990
When it rains people go indoors.

00:32:30.990 --> 00:32:32.430
They watch more TV.

00:32:32.430 --> 00:32:34.920
They generate more
diagnostic events.

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:37.140
Also we can have news events.

00:32:37.140 --> 00:32:39.240
We can have sports events.

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:42.190
And on those days we get
more diagnostic events.

00:32:42.190 --> 00:32:45.600
So the problem with
our fixed-size system

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:47.400
is that we have
to over-provision,

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:51.000
so effectively if
we're provisioning

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:54.960
to the yellow line, we have a
lot of capacity that's wasted.

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:58.990
Whereas, in worse case, we
under-provision the system.

00:32:58.990 --> 00:33:02.430
And that means our batch
processing or real-time jobs

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:03.510
take longer.

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:07.410
Or worst case, we
lose data altogether.

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:08.820
The other problem
that we had was

00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:12.120
that we spent a lot of time
changing our Hadoop cluster.

00:33:12.120 --> 00:33:15.210
I don't know how many people
in the audience run Hadoop,

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:19.410
but our ops team spent time
tuning YARN on a regular basis

00:33:19.410 --> 00:33:22.040
and dealing with
managing YARN containers.

00:33:22.040 --> 00:33:24.700
We had a platform
with mixed workloads,

00:33:24.700 --> 00:33:27.930
so it's very difficult to
get those workflows correct

00:33:27.930 --> 00:33:32.170
when things changed over time.

00:33:32.170 --> 00:33:35.040
So not a big surprise,
we moved to GCP.

00:33:35.040 --> 00:33:38.850
And our architecture is designed
around Pub/Sub, Cloud Dataflow,

00:33:38.850 --> 00:33:40.860
and BigQuery.

00:33:40.860 --> 00:33:43.140
The reason that we
chose this is it's

00:33:43.140 --> 00:33:46.470
Google's canonical architecture,
so really good integration

00:33:46.470 --> 00:33:48.810
between all those components.

00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:52.440
And we took the opportunity to
move the pipeline from batch

00:33:52.440 --> 00:33:57.120
processing to streaming because
our modern Q boxes report back

00:33:57.120 --> 00:33:59.490
in real time over IP.

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:00.990
And the other thing
is that we chose

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:04.670
to use Dataflow to gain the
benefits of autoscaling,

00:34:04.670 --> 00:34:08.400
serverless architecture, and
out-of-order data processing.

00:34:08.400 --> 00:34:12.150
So much like Ryan's
example with mobile gaming,

00:34:12.150 --> 00:34:15.300
people turn off our
TV boxes at night.

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:17.929
Quite often we get
data turning up late.

00:34:17.929 --> 00:34:23.850
So out-of-order data processing
is really important to us.

00:34:23.850 --> 00:34:31.239
So features in the Beam
model, really important,

00:34:31.239 --> 00:34:34.659
and we also think the Beam model
is becoming much more mature.

00:34:34.659 --> 00:34:36.210
And we like the
sort of separation

00:34:36.210 --> 00:34:38.659
between the runner and the SDK.

00:34:38.659 --> 00:34:42.520
I think it's a good thing
to be able to abstract

00:34:42.520 --> 00:34:44.527
the data pipeline itself
from the infrastructure

00:34:44.527 --> 00:34:45.110
or the runner.

00:34:47.750 --> 00:34:50.780
So moving on to talk
about our architecture,

00:34:50.780 --> 00:34:54.880
effectively what we do here is
we take data in streaming mode,

00:34:54.880 --> 00:34:55.969
in the ingest stage.

00:34:55.969 --> 00:35:02.240
So it's JSON data, just like
in the mobile gaming example.

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:06.290
We take it from our
Q box into Pub/Sub.

00:35:06.290 --> 00:35:12.290
Separately we take reference
data, batch mode into GCS.

00:35:12.290 --> 00:35:15.230
So then in our process
stage, what we do

00:35:15.230 --> 00:35:17.030
is process this in Dataflow.

00:35:17.030 --> 00:35:19.970
So in the streaming
path, we clean the data.

00:35:19.970 --> 00:35:22.100
We add additional attributes.

00:35:22.100 --> 00:35:23.860
We assign join IDs.

00:35:23.860 --> 00:35:27.460
We sessionize the data,
so session windows.

00:35:27.460 --> 00:35:30.830
We turn a sequence
of user events

00:35:30.830 --> 00:35:33.380
into distinct TV sessions.

00:35:33.380 --> 00:35:37.130
And these are enriched with
session IDs, start times,

00:35:37.130 --> 00:35:40.320
and the event
number of sessions.

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:45.770
So we think that the
ability to dynamically add

00:35:45.770 --> 00:35:48.350
columns in BigQuery
when our JSON schema

00:35:48.350 --> 00:35:50.570
changes is a great feature,
and we make use of this.

00:35:50.570 --> 00:35:53.660
So this means we can
have non-breaking changes

00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:55.380
with the source system.

00:35:55.380 --> 00:35:57.950
So our source system
team just adds an element

00:35:57.950 --> 00:36:02.050
to the JSON message, and that
manifests itself in BigQuery

00:36:02.050 --> 00:36:04.590
as a new column.

00:36:04.590 --> 00:36:06.560
So what we've got
is also a separation

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:10.400
between our first stage of
processing and enrichment.

00:36:10.400 --> 00:36:11.930
And the reason
that we do this is

00:36:11.930 --> 00:36:16.500
that we use our granular
data for multiple use cases.

00:36:16.500 --> 00:36:21.230
And we don't always want to be
able to use the enriched data

00:36:21.230 --> 00:36:22.840
in those separate use cases.

00:36:22.840 --> 00:36:26.210
So we have two stages of
processing in our architecture.

00:36:26.210 --> 00:36:28.910
So in the enriched
stage, what we do is

00:36:28.910 --> 00:36:32.840
we create this notion
of Q box journeys.

00:36:32.840 --> 00:36:35.060
And these box
journeys add things

00:36:35.060 --> 00:36:37.040
like the customer details.

00:36:37.040 --> 00:36:40.910
They add information around
how the planner works

00:36:40.910 --> 00:36:43.370
and different
reference data relating

00:36:43.370 --> 00:36:45.470
to the tiles on the UI.

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:48.890
So for example, we can see
then that somebody started

00:36:48.890 --> 00:36:51.470
searching for data on the box.

00:36:51.470 --> 00:36:53.420
They found "Game of Thrones."

00:36:53.420 --> 00:36:55.430
And then they started
watching "Game of Thrones"

00:36:55.430 --> 00:37:00.590
and engaged in a big "Game
of Thrones" TV marathon.

00:37:00.590 --> 00:37:05.260
Finally, we display
all this in Tableau.

00:37:05.260 --> 00:37:07.130
So we've got a
number of aggregates

00:37:07.130 --> 00:37:12.620
that sit under Tableau and allow
Tableau to perform effectively

00:37:12.620 --> 00:37:17.550
and give everyone really
kind of snappy performance.

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:21.500
So again, like in the example
that Ryan was talking about,

00:37:21.500 --> 00:37:25.320
we make use of sessioning
for our TV events.

00:37:25.320 --> 00:37:28.340
So Sky processes sessions
based on a continuous stream

00:37:28.340 --> 00:37:31.130
of diagnostic events.

00:37:31.130 --> 00:37:36.340
Each one of those is calculated
for an individual Sky Q box.

00:37:36.340 --> 00:37:41.169
And we do this based on a
four-hour gap of inactivity.

00:37:41.169 --> 00:37:43.460
So essentially what we're
looking for is that four-hour

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:47.660
gap of activity and then
everything in that window

00:37:47.660 --> 00:37:49.860
before it becomes a session.

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:52.670
We've got a hard cutoff
after 24 hours on the basis

00:37:52.670 --> 00:37:56.180
that we think no one watches
TV for more than 24 hours.

00:38:00.160 --> 00:38:02.700
So we also make
use of autoscaling.

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:05.860
So autoscaling
happens transparently,

00:38:05.860 --> 00:38:08.790
but interestingly the
rules for autoscaling

00:38:08.790 --> 00:38:10.030
are quite sophisticated.

00:38:10.030 --> 00:38:13.740
So don't expect autoscaling to
happen immediately when there's

00:38:13.740 --> 00:38:16.980
an influx of new work to do.

00:38:16.980 --> 00:38:21.340
Sometimes it takes a while
for Dataflow to scale up,

00:38:21.340 --> 00:38:24.480
and that's because it's got
a sophisticated heuristic

00:38:24.480 --> 00:38:27.180
in the background
working and optimizing

00:38:27.180 --> 00:38:30.730
the number of workers in
the Dataflow pipeline.

00:38:30.730 --> 00:38:32.940
So essentially, it
might add workers

00:38:32.940 --> 00:38:35.310
to keep up with the input rate.

00:38:35.310 --> 00:38:37.830
So it knows the amounts
of unprocessed work

00:38:37.830 --> 00:38:41.160
in Pub/Sub or the queue depth.

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:43.422
And it might also need to
catch up with the backlog.

00:38:43.422 --> 00:38:44.880
And it's smart
enough to understand

00:38:44.880 --> 00:38:47.550
the rate of processing
and whether it's worth

00:38:47.550 --> 00:38:50.970
scaling up those extra
workers to process

00:38:50.970 --> 00:38:52.440
that backlog of work.

00:38:52.440 --> 00:38:54.540
So sometimes it
might not scale up

00:38:54.540 --> 00:38:58.110
because it will realize that
by the time you've scaled up

00:38:58.110 --> 00:39:00.270
and added more workers,
the work would be done.

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:06.050
So what we've got
now is a situation

00:39:06.050 --> 00:39:11.390
where we can process many
hundreds of millions of records

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:14.240
without having to manage
any of the infrastructure

00:39:14.240 --> 00:39:18.240
and be able to cope with those
peaks entirely dynamically.

00:39:18.240 --> 00:39:22.850
So we're regularly processing
more than 300 million messages

00:39:22.850 --> 00:39:28.610
per day, in some cases
now well over 400 million.

00:39:28.610 --> 00:39:31.370
It's been a great
success in the business.

00:39:31.370 --> 00:39:34.370
It only took five weeks to
develop this solution from end

00:39:34.370 --> 00:39:35.670
to end.

00:39:35.670 --> 00:39:37.490
And we agonized a
bit about whether we

00:39:37.490 --> 00:39:42.530
should port our existing Apache
Spark code over and run it

00:39:42.530 --> 00:39:44.150
on Dataproc.

00:39:44.150 --> 00:39:45.920
And we think we made
the right decision

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:49.880
not to do that and rewrite
everything in Dataflow, just

00:39:49.880 --> 00:39:52.490
because we've taken
all the benefit of not

00:39:52.490 --> 00:39:54.540
having to manage the cluster.

00:39:54.540 --> 00:39:57.140
We also partnered
with Datatonic, who

00:39:57.140 --> 00:39:58.640
we may have in the audience.

00:39:58.640 --> 00:40:01.880
But Datatonic are experts in
data engineering and machine

00:40:01.880 --> 00:40:02.820
learning.

00:40:02.820 --> 00:40:04.640
And they were able
to get our team up

00:40:04.640 --> 00:40:11.480
to speed with Apache Beam,
Pub/Sub, BigQuery really

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:13.070
quickly.

00:40:13.070 --> 00:40:15.470
We do A/B testing
on the box software,

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:18.682
so we can determine whether,
from one release of the Q

00:40:18.682 --> 00:40:20.390
software to the next,
that we're actually

00:40:20.390 --> 00:40:24.660
seeing material benefits and
improvements in the software.

00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:26.870
So typically what
we do is we take

00:40:26.870 --> 00:40:30.590
a small population of boxes,
we roll those boxes up

00:40:30.590 --> 00:40:33.410
to the new revision,
and we watch

00:40:33.410 --> 00:40:37.830
whether both old and
new cohorts of box users

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:39.870
are performing as
we would expect

00:40:39.870 --> 00:40:43.910
and whether the new cohort
is seeing a material benefit.

00:40:43.910 --> 00:40:46.280
And in many cases,
we can now find

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:49.490
that we're seeing more than
double-digit improvements

00:40:49.490 --> 00:40:55.080
in performance of the
UI and user experience.

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:58.260
So a quick summary
of before and after--

00:40:58.260 --> 00:41:01.490
so before we had an
on-prem Hadoop cluster.

00:41:01.490 --> 00:41:06.170
We had to manage network,
servers, software upgrades.

00:41:06.170 --> 00:41:07.880
We had to patch everything.

00:41:07.880 --> 00:41:09.350
We had a lot to maintain.

00:41:09.350 --> 00:41:14.000
Now we've got a completely
serverless as a service model.

00:41:14.000 --> 00:41:19.760
We can scale in no time
at all, like 90 seconds,

00:41:19.760 --> 00:41:20.720
to add more workers.

00:41:20.720 --> 00:41:23.150
Whereas before, we
were talking months

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:26.710
to add new servers, where we may
have to even put in a request

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:31.280
to finance and deal with all of
the capital expenditure outlay.

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:34.285
So it's high fixed costs
with the existing solution,

00:41:34.285 --> 00:41:37.820
and now we can just
pay for what we use.

00:41:37.820 --> 00:41:41.990
One of the biggest improvements
has been in development time.

00:41:41.990 --> 00:41:44.600
Before, with the fixed-size
system on-premise,

00:41:44.600 --> 00:41:47.870
it was almost impossible
to recreate that production

00:41:47.870 --> 00:41:50.540
workload, especially with
multiple workloads running

00:41:50.540 --> 00:41:52.260
on the same system.

00:41:52.260 --> 00:41:54.020
So development time
took a long time

00:41:54.020 --> 00:41:57.500
because it was very difficult
to test things and replicate

00:41:57.500 --> 00:41:59.120
the production workload.

00:41:59.120 --> 00:42:03.170
Now we can replay all our
messages back into Pub/Sub,

00:42:03.170 --> 00:42:07.550
through into Dataflow and
into the new developed

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:08.840
version of our pipeline.

00:42:08.840 --> 00:42:12.086
And it's very much, very
much simpler to test.

00:42:12.086 --> 00:42:13.460
And that's meant
that development

00:42:13.460 --> 00:42:16.970
now happening in
weeks, not months.

00:42:16.970 --> 00:42:18.980
And ops are delighted.

00:42:18.980 --> 00:42:22.380
Rather than spending two
days a week tuning YARN,

00:42:22.380 --> 00:42:23.780
its near zero ops.

00:42:27.140 --> 00:42:33.110
So again, great success for
Sky and also a great success

00:42:33.110 --> 00:42:34.000
for the business.

00:42:34.000 --> 00:42:37.890
So Dan Chronnell, who heads
up our product experience,

00:42:37.890 --> 00:42:43.550
now feels that this is an
extension of the product design

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:45.530
and development capability.

00:42:45.530 --> 00:42:47.840
So he says, "This
capability has the power

00:42:47.840 --> 00:42:50.630
to change the way we
develop our products

00:42:50.630 --> 00:42:54.900
and has made me very proud of
what we've built together."

00:42:54.900 --> 00:42:57.620
And finally, I'd just like
to say a couple of thank yous

00:42:57.620 --> 00:43:00.500
to Datatonic, who partnered
with us to build this,

00:43:00.500 --> 00:43:03.390
and also Agile
Solutions, who helped

00:43:03.390 --> 00:43:04.850
do some of the
project management.

00:43:04.850 --> 00:43:07.820
So thanks to Louis and
Andrew on the technical side

00:43:07.820 --> 00:43:10.940
at Datatonic, and Stephen
from Agile Solutions.

00:43:10.940 --> 00:43:13.990
[MUSIC PLAYING]

